IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `intl-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for International Enforcement and Cooperation (2017), section 4.2:
-- consideration of foreign government involvement.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. "In determining whether to conduct an investigation or
-- to file an enforcement action in cases in which foreign government involvement is known or
-- suspected, the Agencies consider four legal doctrines" (4.2 [pdf page 32]). Each doctrine
-- keeps its own modal, and nothing here lends one doctrine's words to another:
--   4.2.1 foreign sovereign immunity: what the FSIA does in civil cases, as the text states it;
--   4.2.2 foreign sovereign compulsion: the agencies "will refrain ... only when certain
--     criteria are satisfied", and Example E's "would acknowledge a defense";
--   4.2.3 act of state: the agencies "may exercise enforcement discretion and decline";
--   4.2.4 petitioning of sovereigns: the agencies "will not challenge" genuine efforts, and
--     "will not exercise this discretion" for a sham.
-- n.109 [pdf page 32]: "investigation may be necessary to assess the nature of foreign
-- government involvement ... even where an Agency ultimately refrains from enforcement."

§ `Section 4.2: foreign government involvement`

§§ `4.2.1 Foreign sovereign immunity`

-- n.112 [pdf page 33]: the FSIA "defines 'foreign state' to include a 'political subdivision
-- of a foreign state or an agency or instrumentality of a foreign state'", and defines an
-- agency or instrumentality "to mean any entity" that (1) is a separate legal person, (2) is
-- an organ of a foreign state or subdivision, or majority-owned by one, and (3) is neither a
-- citizen of a State of the United States nor created under the laws of a third country.
-- Majority ownership needs the "foreign state itself" to own the majority (Dole Food). "To
-- include" is open, so an entity that is none of these is NOTHING, not FALSE (fork F10). The
-- FSIA does not apply to individual foreign officials: FALSE.
@ref n.112 [pdf page 33]; 4.2.1 [pdf page 33]
GIVEN x IS A `Entity before the FSIA (4.2.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`4.2.1: the entity is a foreign state under the FSIA` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF    x's `the kind of entity` EQUALS `a foreign state itself`
         OR x's `the kind of entity` EQUALS `a political subdivision of a foreign state`
      THEN JUST TRUE
      IF x's `the kind of entity` EQUALS `an individual foreign official`
      THEN JUST FALSE
      IF     x's `it is a separate legal person, corporate or otherwise`
         AND (    x's `it is an organ of a foreign state or political subdivision of one`
               OR x's `a majority of its shares or other ownership interest is owned by the foreign state itself or a political subdivision of it`)
         AND (NOT x's `it is a citizen of a State of the United States, or created under the laws of a third country`)
      THEN JUST TRUE
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- 4.2.1 [pdf pages 33-34]: "the commercial character of an activity shall be determined by
-- reference to the nature of the course of conduct or particular transaction or act, rather
-- than by reference to its purpose"; "the principal question is whether the government is
-- acting 'not as a regulator of a market, but in the manner of a private player within
-- it.'" The purpose is read by no rule.
@ref 4.2.1 [pdf page 34]; n.117 to n.119 [pdf page 34]
GIVEN a IS A `Action against a foreign state (4.2.1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `4.2.1: the activity is commercial` IF
    a's `in the activity, the foreign state acts in the manner of a private player within a market, not as a regulator of it`

-- 4.2.1 [pdf page 34]: the three limbs of the commercial activity exception, as the text
-- quotes 28 U.S.C. 1605(a)(2) (n.116).
@ref 4.2.1 [pdf pages 33-34]; n.115, n.116 [pdf page 34]
GIVEN a IS A `Action against a foreign state (4.2.1)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `4.2.1: the commercial activity exception applies` IF
        `4.2.1: the activity is commercial` a
    AND (    a's `the action is based upon the activity carried on in the United States by the foreign state, with substantial contact with the United States`
          OR a's `the action is based upon an act performed in the United States in connection with the activity of the foreign state elsewhere`
          OR a's `the action is based upon an act outside the territory of the United States in connection with the activity of the foreign state elsewhere, and that act causes a direct effect in the United States`)

DECLARE `What section 4.2.1 says of immunity` IS ONE OF
    `the FSIA does not apply: the immunity of an individual foreign official is governed by the common law`
    `the foreign state is not immune from the jurisdiction of U.S. courts: the commercial activity exception`
    `the FSIA shields the foreign state from the civil jurisdiction of U.S. courts, subject to its other enumerated exceptions and to treaties in place at the time of its enactment`

-- 4.2.1 [pdf page 33]: "In civil cases", the FSIA "shields foreign states ... from the civil
-- jurisdiction of the courts of the United States, subject to certain enumerated exceptions
-- and to treaties in place at the time of the FSIA's enactment"; "The 'commercial activity'
-- exception is the most relevant exception for antitrust purposes", and under it "a foreign
-- state is not immune". The other exceptions (a, c to f) are listed without their
-- conditions, so the shield is reported with its qualifier. A non-civil case, and an entity
-- the FSIA's definition does not reach, are NOTHING: the text speaks of neither. The text
-- states no burden on a defendant here (fork F26; NOTES.md section 10).
@ref 4.2.1 [pdf pages 33-35]; n.110 to n.116
GIVEN x IS A `Entity before the FSIA (4.2.1)`
      a IS A `Action against a foreign state (4.2.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2.1 says of immunity`
`4.2.1: foreign sovereign immunity` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF x's `the kind of entity` EQUALS `an individual foreign official`
      THEN JUST `the FSIA does not apply: the immunity of an individual foreign official is governed by the common law`
      IF NOT a's `the action is a civil case`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF NOT (`4.2.1: the entity is a foreign state under the FSIA` x EQUALS JUST TRUE)
      THEN NOTHING
      IF `4.2.1: the commercial activity exception applies` a
      THEN JUST `the foreign state is not immune from the jurisdiction of U.S. courts: the commercial activity exception`
      OTHERWISE JUST `the FSIA shields the foreign state from the civil jurisdiction of U.S. courts, subject to its other enumerated exceptions and to treaties in place at the time of its enactment`

DECLARE `What section 4.2.1 says of a state-owned enterprise` IS ONE OF
    `the commercial activities of the enterprise are subject to the U.S. antitrust laws to the same extent as the activities of privately owned foreign firms`

-- 4.2.1 [pdf page 35]: "As a practical matter, most activities of foreign state-owned
-- enterprises operating in the commercial marketplace are 'commercial' ... The commercial
-- activities of these enterprises are subject to the U.S. antitrust laws to the same extent
-- as the activities of privately owned foreign firms." "Most" is not encoded as a rule: the
-- activity must be found commercial. A state-owned enterprise is read as the third kind of
-- entity, majority-owned by the foreign state itself (fork F10).
@ref 4.2.1 [pdf page 35]; n.112 [pdf page 33]
GIVEN x IS A `Entity before the FSIA (4.2.1)`
      a IS A `Action against a foreign state (4.2.1)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2.1 says of a state-owned enterprise`
`4.2.1: the commercial activities of a state-owned enterprise` MEANS
    IF     x's `the kind of entity` EQUALS `another entity, which may be an agency or instrumentality of a foreign state`
       AND x's `a majority of its shares or other ownership interest is owned by the foreign state itself or a political subdivision of it`
       AND `4.2.1: the activity is commercial` a
    THEN JUST `the commercial activities of the enterprise are subject to the U.S. antitrust laws to the same extent as the activities of privately owned foreign firms`
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `4.2.2 Foreign sovereign compulsion`

DECLARE `How the agencies regard a formal representation of compulsion (4.2.2)` IS ONE OF
    `as a general matter, the agencies regard the representation as sufficient to establish that the conduct has been compelled`
    `the representation is not sufficient: it lacks the detail to see precisely how the compulsion would be accomplished under foreign law`
    `the agencies may not regard the statement as dispositive, and may inquire into its circumstances or request further information`

-- 4.2.2 [pdf page 36]: "As a general matter, the Agencies regard the foreign government's
-- formal representation that refusal to comply with its command would have such a result as
-- being sufficient to establish that the conduct in question has been compelled. To be
-- sufficient, however, the representation must contain enough detail to enable the Agencies
-- to see precisely how the compulsion would be accomplished under foreign law." n.124: "the
-- Agencies may not regard as dispositive a statement that is unsupported or ambiguous, or
-- that, on its face, appears to be internally inconsistent." Detail is read first ("must");
-- then n.124's "may not"; then the general matter. No representation: NOTHING.
@ref 4.2.2 [pdf page 36]; n.124 [pdf page 36]
GIVEN r IS A `Formal representation of compulsion (4.2.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `How the agencies regard a formal representation of compulsion (4.2.2)`
`4.2.2: how the agencies regard the representation` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT r's `the foreign government has formally represented that refusal to comply with its command would give rise to penal or other severe sanctions`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF NOT r's `the representation contains enough detail to enable the agencies to see precisely how the compulsion would be accomplished under foreign law`
      THEN JUST `the representation is not sufficient: it lacks the detail to see precisely how the compulsion would be accomplished under foreign law`
      IF r's `the statement is unsupported or ambiguous, or appears on its face to be internally inconsistent`
      THEN JUST `the agencies may not regard the statement as dispositive, and may inquire into its circumstances or request further information`
      OTHERWISE JUST `as a general matter, the agencies regard the representation as sufficient to establish that the conduct has been compelled`

-- 4.2.2 [pdf pages 35-37]: each statement of the text that a set of facts can engage against
-- the defense, in the text's order.
DECLARE `Statement against the defense of foreign sovereign compulsion (4.2.2)` IS ONE OF
    `the first criterion is not met: the foreign government has not compelled the conduct`
    `foreign government measures short of compulsion do not suffice for this defense, although they may be a relevant comity consideration`
    `discretionary conduct is outside the protections afforded by this defense`
    `if it is possible for a party to comply with both the foreign law and the U.S. antitrust laws, the existence of the foreign law does not provide any legal excuse for actions that do not comply with U.S. law`
    `if the compelled conduct occurs in the United States, the agencies will not recognize the defense`
    `the defense generally applies only when the compelled conduct can be accomplished entirely within the foreign sovereign's own territory`
    `the order must come from the foreign government acting in its governmental capacity`
    `the defense does not arise from conduct that would fall within the FSIA commercial activity exception`

-- 4.2.2 [pdf pages 35-37], n.122, n.123. That the conduct is lawful, approved or encouraged
-- abroad, or that the foreign jurisdiction strongly favours it, "does not, in and of itself,
-- bar application of the U.S. antitrust laws": no statement here reads either.
@ref 4.2.2 [pdf pages 35-37]; n.121 to n.126 [pdf pages 35-37]
GIVEN s IS A `Facts of foreign sovereign compulsion (4.2.2)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Statement against the defense of foreign sovereign compulsion (4.2.2)`
`4.2.2: the statements against the defense that the facts engage` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF s's `the measure` EQUALS `no measure of the foreign government` THEN JUST `the first criterion is not met: the foreign government has not compelled the conduct` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `the measure` EQUALS `a measure short of compulsion` THEN JUST `foreign government measures short of compulsion do not suffice for this defense, although they may be a relevant comity consideration` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `the conduct is discretionary` THEN JUST `discretionary conduct is outside the protections afforded by this defense` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `the party can comply with both the foreign law and the U.S. antitrust laws` THEN JUST `if it is possible for a party to comply with both the foreign law and the U.S. antitrust laws, the existence of the foreign law does not provide any legal excuse for actions that do not comply with U.S. law` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `where the compelled conduct is accomplished` EQUALS `in the United States` THEN JUST `if the compelled conduct occurs in the United States, the agencies will not recognize the defense` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `where the compelled conduct is accomplished` EQUALS `not entirely within the foreign sovereign's own territory, and not in the United States` THEN JUST `the defense generally applies only when the compelled conduct can be accomplished entirely within the foreign sovereign's own territory` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF NOT s's `the order comes from the foreign government acting in its governmental capacity` THEN JUST `the order must come from the foreign government acting in its governmental capacity` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF s's `the conduct would fall within the FSIA commercial activity exception` THEN JUST `the defense does not arise from conduct that would fall within the FSIA commercial activity exception` ELSE NOTHING))

DECLARE `What section 4.2.2 says of foreign sovereign compulsion` IS ONE OF
    `the criteria are satisfied: the agencies would acknowledge a defense of sovereign compulsion`
    `the criteria are not satisfied: the agencies will not refrain from an enforcement action based on considerations of foreign sovereign compulsion, and would also consider whether comity factors counsel against one`

-- 4.2.2 [pdf page 36]: "the Agencies will refrain from bringing an enforcement action based
-- on considerations of foreign sovereign compulsion only when certain criteria are
-- satisfied." When a criterion fails, that is the text's no. When every criterion is met,
-- the "only when" alone would say nothing; Example E [pdf page 37] says that then "The
-- Agencies would acknowledge a defense of sovereign compulsion", and that result keeps the
-- Example's words (fork F11). n.127: "As in all such cases, the Agencies would also consider
-- whether comity factors counsel against bringing an enforcement action." The second
-- criterion holds "generally": conduct neither entirely within the foreign territory nor in
-- the United States, with nothing else against the defense, is NOTHING (fork F12).
@ref 4.2.2 [pdf pages 35-37]; Illustrative Example E [pdf page 37]; n.127 [pdf page 37]
GIVEN s IS A `Facts of foreign sovereign compulsion (4.2.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2.2 says of foreign sovereign compulsion`
`4.2.2: foreign sovereign compulsion` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF null (`4.2.2: the statements against the defense that the facts engage` s)
      THEN JUST `the criteria are satisfied: the agencies would acknowledge a defense of sovereign compulsion`
      IF `4.2.2: the statements against the defense that the facts engage` s EQUALS LIST `the defense generally applies only when the compelled conduct can be accomplished entirely within the foreign sovereign's own territory`
      THEN NOTHING
      OTHERWISE JUST `the criteria are not satisfied: the agencies will not refrain from an enforcement action based on considerations of foreign sovereign compulsion, and would also consider whether comity factors counsel against one`

§§ `4.2.3 Act of state doctrine`

DECLARE `What section 4.2.3 says of an act of state` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies may exercise enforcement discretion and decline to challenge the foreign act of state`
    `the doctrine does not apply to the acts of individual government officials acting outside their official capacity`
    `the doctrine does not apply to private actors, even when their acts are approved or condoned by the foreign government`

-- 4.2.3 [pdf pages 38-39]: the doctrine "does not apply to the acts of individual government
-- officials acting outside their official capacity. Nor does it apply to private actors,
-- even when those acts are approved or condoned by the foreign government in question." The
-- agencies "may exercise enforcement discretion and decline to challenge foreign acts of
-- state if the facts and circumstances indicate that: (1) the specific conduct complained of
-- is a public act of the sovereign, (2) the act was taken within the territorial
-- jurisdiction of the sovereign, and (3) the conduct relates to a matter that is
-- governmental, rather than commercial." A sufficient condition only: when one fails, the
-- text states nothing (NOTHING); it does not say the agencies will challenge.
@ref 4.2.3 [pdf pages 38-39]; n.128 to n.133 [pdf pages 38-39]
GIVEN a IS A `Facts of an act of state (4.2.3)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2.3 says of an act of state`
`4.2.3: act of state` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF a's `the actor` EQUALS `an individual government official acting outside the official capacity`
      THEN JUST `the doctrine does not apply to the acts of individual government officials acting outside their official capacity`
      IF a's `the actor` EQUALS `a private actor`
      THEN JUST `the doctrine does not apply to private actors, even when their acts are approved or condoned by the foreign government`
      IF     a's `the specific conduct complained of is a public act of the sovereign`
         AND a's `the act was taken within the territorial jurisdiction of the sovereign`
         AND a's `the conduct relates to a matter that is governmental, rather than commercial`
      THEN JUST `the agencies may exercise enforcement discretion and decline to challenge the foreign act of state`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `4.2.4 Petitioning of sovereigns`

DECLARE `What section 4.2.4 says of petitioning` IS ONE OF
    `under the Noerr-Pennington doctrine, a genuine effort to obtain or influence action by governmental entities in the United States falls outside the scope of the Sherman Act`
    `the agencies will not challenge under the antitrust laws the genuine effort to obtain or influence action by the foreign government entity`
    `the agencies will not exercise this discretion: the petitioning is a sham`
    `the agencies will not exercise this discretion: Noerr-Pennington would otherwise not apply`

-- 4.2.4 [pdf page 39]: "Under the Noerr-Pennington doctrine, a genuine effort to obtain or
-- influence action by governmental entities in the United States falls outside the scope of
-- the Sherman Act ... The Agencies, therefore, will not challenge under the antitrust laws
-- genuine efforts to obtain or influence action by foreign government entities. But as with
-- Noerr-Pennington, the Agencies will not exercise this discretion when faced with 'sham'
-- activities ... or when Noerr-Pennington would otherwise not apply." A genuine effort is one
-- that is not a sham (fork F14). For a U.S. entity, only the genuine effort is stated; a
-- sham or a case where the doctrine would not apply is NOTHING there. No effort: NOTHING.
-- The intent field is read by no rule ("even if the intent or effect of that effort is to
-- restrain or monopolize trade").
@ref 4.2.4 [pdf page 39]; n.134 to n.137 [pdf page 39]
GIVEN p IS A `Facts of petitioning (4.2.4)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2.4 says of petitioning`
`4.2.4: petitioning of sovereigns` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF NOT p's `the conduct is an effort to obtain or influence action by the government entity`
      THEN NOTHING
      IF p's `the government entity petitioned` EQUALS `a governmental entity in the United States`
      THEN IF    p's `the petitioning, ostensibly directed toward influencing governmental action, is a mere sham to cover an attempt to interfere directly with the business relationships of a competitor`
              OR p's `Noerr-Pennington would otherwise not apply`
           THEN NOTHING
           ELSE JUST `under the Noerr-Pennington doctrine, a genuine effort to obtain or influence action by governmental entities in the United States falls outside the scope of the Sherman Act`
      IF p's `the petitioning, ostensibly directed toward influencing governmental action, is a mere sham to cover an attempt to interfere directly with the business relationships of a competitor`
      THEN JUST `the agencies will not exercise this discretion: the petitioning is a sham`
      IF p's `Noerr-Pennington would otherwise not apply`
      THEN JUST `the agencies will not exercise this discretion: Noerr-Pennington would otherwise not apply`
      OTHERWISE JUST `the agencies will not challenge under the antitrust laws the genuine effort to obtain or influence action by the foreign government entity`
